<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Primary adrenal insufficiency (autoimmune Addison&apos;s disease) (E27.1). The low cortisol with a markedly raised ACTH before treatment, with low sodium and high potassium, establishes primary adrenal insufficiency, and the positive 21-hydroxylase antibodies establish an autoimmune cause. A cosyntropin test would need treatment to be stopped and adds nothing; adrenal imaging and other causes need not be pursued with positive antibodies. Glucocorticoid replacement is more than he needs at 30 mg a day; mineralocorticoid replacement is too low, shown by salt craving, the postural drop, the electrolytes and the raised renin. [S]</item><item>Raised TSH with positive thyroid peroxidase antibodies (R94.6). Probable autoimmune thyroiditis, which often accompanies autoimmune adrenal insufficiency. A raised TSH can settle once glucocorticoid replacement is established, but he has been on replacement for three months, so this is likely to be true thyroid disease. A newly raised TSH should be confirmed before treatment; levothyroxine is safe to start because glucocorticoid replacement is in place, and it must never be started before it. [S]</item><item>Chronic low back pain; bone health (M96.1). His Z-scores are within the expected range for his age, so there is no sign of bone loss from his earlier corticosteroids. Physiological replacement at 15 to 25 mg of hydrocortisone a day does not need bone-protective medicine; over-replacement is what should be avoided. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Prescott&apos;s intake note and the hospital discharge letter (low morning cortisol with markedly raised ACTH before treatment, low sodium, high potassium). Intake results: sodium 134, potassium 5.2 mmol/L; creatinine 0.96 mg/dL; plasma renin activity 9.6 ng/mL/h; 21-hydroxylase antibodies 18 U/mL; TSH 7.4 mIU/L, free T4 1.0 ng/dL, thyroid peroxidase antibodies 286 IU/mL; vitamin B12 455 pg/mL; A1c 5.1%; tissue transglutaminase IgA 1.1 U/mL with total IgA 214 mg/dL; 25-hydroxyvitamin D 31 ng/mL. Bone density scan this morning: lumbar spine Z-score -1.3, total hip Z-score -0.6. Weight 66.8 kg today, from 66.0. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>No further diagnostic testing for the adrenal insufficiency: the cause is autoimmune. [S]</item><item>Reduce hydrocortisone to 10 mg on waking, 5 mg at noon and 5 mg at 4 pm (20 mg a day), adjusted by weight, energy and signs of over- or under-replacement rather than by cortisol levels. [S]</item><item>Increase fludrocortisone to 0.1 mg once daily; check sodium, potassium, renin and lying and standing blood pressure in about six weeks, aiming for renin in the upper normal range. [S]</item><item>Repeat TSH and free T4 at the six-week visit; if the TSH is still raised, start levothyroxine 50 mcg daily and check TSH six weeks later. [S]</item><item>No bone-protective medicine; calcium and vitamin D from diet, weight-bearing exercise, and no repeat scan unless his dose rises or he has a fracture. [S]</item><item>Sick-day rules: double the hydrocortisone for fever above 38 °C and triple it above 39 °C until a day after the fever settles; inject 100 mg into the thigh and call 911 for vomiting, inability to take tablets, or feeling faint. For surgery or dental work under general anesthesia, or a major illness in hospital, hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously at induction and then 200 mg a day by infusion or divided doses until he is eating, tapering back over one to two days. [S]</item><item>Carry the injection kit, a steroid emergency card and a medical ID at all times, with spare tablets when traveling. [S]</item><item>Screen for associated autoimmune disease: TSH at least yearly, and vitamin B12, A1c and celiac serology yearly or with symptoms. [S]</item><item>Endocrinology review at least yearly. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Prescott, who sees him the same day. The plan and the sick-day rules were explained to Mr. Kennedy, who agreed and demonstrated a practice injection; he decides who else receives this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Endocrinology review in one year, or sooner for an adrenal crisis or a change in the thyroid results; Dr. Prescott continues prescribing and monitoring and will share the six-week results. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
